--- A function only handed in a group — never called by the program — is
--- typed by what its body reads (spec, sys: two operations over functions):
--- `upper[x]` reads its parameter as text, and what `describe` says of it
--- says so. Only a call had typed one, and a function read in its body
--- was refused as typed by nothing, the refusal's own fix ("read it in
--- the body") doing nothing. A member of the group is applied by name to
--- arguments in a parsed value, as a model's call names them. (Held over
--- llm's `chat` until llm moved to its own repository, 2026-11-07.)
use sys

--- says its argument back, louder
fn shout[x]
    return upper[x]

--- what each function is said to take, and what it answers the arguments
fn told[tools, given]
    said = []
    for t in tools
        d = describe[t]
        out = apply[t, given]
        if out.ok
            said = push[said, '`str[d.name]` takes `str[d.params[0].takes]` and answers `out`']
        else
            said = push[said, '`str[d.name]` refused: `out.reason`']
    return said

for line in told[[shout], json['{"x": "hi"}']]
    log[line]
for line in told[[shout], json['{"y": "hi"}']]
    log[line]
